---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { meta } from "../../data/pages";
import sdk from "../../data/sdk.json";
const edgeSource = "https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/60e9fb9362bff7c1635395c7f44ee417aa7440f6";
---
<Layout {...meta("/docs/support/")}>
  <article class="frame prose" style="max-width:var(--page)">
    <p class="eyebrow">Support matrix · formats and harnesses</p>
    <h1 class="display">What works with Torana.</h1>
    <p>Torana runs request and response plugins on recognized model-inference endpoints. Choose a native route for the upstream’s own API, or an opt-in bridge to translate a different client API. “Preserved” in the native matrix below means executable fixtures cover the wire behavior; it does not claim that every model or account enables the provider feature.</p>

    <h2 id="shared-format">One shared format for plugins</h2>
    <p>Torana converts supported provider requests and responses into a shared format, so plugins can work with messages, tools, and usage without parsing each provider’s JSON separately. Technical references call this an <em>intermediate representation</em>, or IR.</p>
    <p>This makes shared plugin logic reusable; it does not make every provider feature interchangeable. On a native route your harness speaks the upstream’s API. With a bridge it speaks the configured <code>bridge.client</code> contract, while Torana translates supported content to <code>bridge.upstream</code>. A plugin may still depend on particular fields or capabilities.</p>

    <h2 id="native">Native provider formats</h2>
    <p>This table applies to routes without a configured bridge. Native feature preservation is broader than the <a href="#bridges">translation surface</a>.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Provider support matrix">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Inference endpoints</th><th>Streaming</th><th>Tools</th><th>Prompt-cache behavior</th><th>Known boundary</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>OpenAI</th><td><code>POST …/chat/completions</code><br/><code>POST …/responses</code></td><td>SSE</td><td>Preserved and mutable under explicit grants</td><td><code>prompt_cache_key</code>, <code>prompt_cache_retention</code>, and usage preserved; Responses compaction is opt-in</td><td>Provider-owned history behind <code>previous_response_id</code> is not rewritten</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Anthropic</th><td><code>POST …/messages</code></td><td>SSE</td><td>Structured use/results and signatures preserved</td><td>Ordered <code>cache_control</code> breakpoints preserved</td><td>Both string and block-array system prompts are accepted</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Gemini / Vertex AI</th><td><code>POST …:generateContent</code><br/><code>POST …:streamGenerateContent</code></td><td>SSE</td><td>Function calls/results, metadata, and signatures preserved</td><td><code>cachedContent</code> references and usage preserved; Torana does not manage cache resources</td><td>Caller-supplied API key or bearer auth; ADC/service-account minting is not built in</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Gemini Code Assist</th><td><code>POST …:generateContent</code><br/><code>POST …:streamGenerateContent</code></td><td>SSE</td><td>Function calls/results and signed parts preserved</td><td>Provider cache references and usage preserved</td><td>Clients without a base-URL setting require Torana’s optional, explicitly configured MITM ingress</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 id="bridges">Opt-in protocol bridges</h2>
    <p>No plugin is required. Configure the client and upstream explicitly using <code>openai-chat</code>, <code>openai-responses</code>, <code>anthropic</code>, <code>gemini</code>, or <code>gemini-codeassist</code>. Provider <code>format</code> describes the upstream family. Cross-family bridges require explicit upstream authentication; caller credentials are not forwarded across families.</p>
    <p>Automated mock-HTTP tests cover all 20 cross-contract directions for common text/function-tool loops and JSON/SSE responses. Image inputs and structured-output constraints require a representable mapping and a capable model. This does not establish compatibility with every live harness or backend.</p>
    <p>Provider-specific reasoning/signatures, server-native tools, cache breakpoints, opaque continuation items, <code>previous_response_id</code>, and storage/background execution are not portable. Unsupported request semantics return 400 before an upstream call. Non-portable response content produces a 502 or terminates an already-started stream without a success marker.</p>
    <p><a href="/docs/protocol-bridges/">Set up a bridge</a> with an Anthropic-client/local-OpenAI example. The <a href={`${edgeSource}/docs/PROTOCOL_BRIDGES.md#supported-translation-surface`}>complete translation reference ↗</a> covers the finer boundaries.</p>

    <h2>Selective interception</h2>
    <p>On native routes, model-list, token-counting, account, quota, status, telemetry, update, MCP, and unknown auxiliary paths pass through without request or response plugins. Real-proxy tests cover that pass-through for all four native formats, including method, path, query, body, permitted headers, upstream status, and response bytes.</p>
    <p>Under any configured bridge, model discovery, token-counting, files, batch APIs, response retrieval, and other auxiliary APIs are not emulated or forwarded; they return 400. This includes bridges whose client and upstream contracts match. Changing a base URL alone does not make a harness that requires these APIs compatible.</p>

    <h2 id="harnesses" data-evidence="automated-contract">Coding harnesses</h2>
    <p>Keep the login you already use. File-read and tool-follow-up checks passed through Torana with Claude Code, Codex, and Antigravity on September 16, 2026, including local usage logging. Pick your recipe in the <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/HARNESS_SETUP.md">harness guide</a> and try it with your own project.</p>
    <p>For Codex response plugins, choose HTTP/SSE with <code>supports_websockets = false</code>: WebSocket connections pass through as opaque streams, outside the response-plugin pipeline. For Antigravity, follow the <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/GEMINI_ANTIGRAVITY.md">local TLS setup</a> and choose your tool permissions; the verified headless run used explicit auto-approval. The guide includes tested versions and setup details.</p>
    <p>The table below maps native routes to their automated API coverage. Connecting a different kind of backend? Start with the <a href="/docs/protocol-bridges/">bridge guide</a>.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Coding harness support matrix">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Harness path</th><th>Torana format</th><th>Automated contract</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Claude Code via <code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code></th><td>Anthropic Messages</td><td>System prompts, streaming tool use/results, cache markers, signatures, and auxiliary pass-through</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Codex custom provider</th><td>OpenAI Responses</td><td>Responses layout, opaque input items, streaming tools, and model-list pass-through</td></tr>
          <tr><th>OpenCode / Aider base URL</th><td>OpenAI or Anthropic</td><td>The selected provider wire has automated coverage</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Antigravity / Code Assist</th><td>Gemini Code Assist</td><td>Wrapper data, signed parts, cache facts, stream framing, and auxiliary pass-through</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <details>
      <summary>About the live checks and API coverage</summary>
      <p>The <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/HARNESS_SETUP.md#live-checks-and-what-they-prove">dated harness results</a> record the versions, models, authentication routes, and workflows checked. Resume, login refresh, and long conversations were not part of those runs; they do not establish every tool or provider account.</p>
      <p>Automated API fixtures exercise protocol behavior, including the 20 bridge directions. They are separate from credentialed harness checks, not evidence that every real harness/backend combination has been run.</p>
    </details>
    <p>Have a favorite harness or a workflow you want to bring to Torana? <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/issues">Share your setup or the first rough edge</a>. Working recipes, bug reports, and small plugins all help shape what comes next.</p>

    <h2>Plugin SDKs</h2>
    <p>SDK <code>{sdk.version}</code> implements ABI major {sdk.abiMajor}, contract revision {sdk.contractRevision}. The SDK package version, plugin ABI, and Edge binary release are separate version numbers. Start with the <a href="/docs/plugin-authoring/">plugin-authoring guide</a> for a project pinned to the host’s SDK.</p>
    <table><tbody>
      <tr><th>Go</th><td>ABI v1 SDK, helpers, native test harness, official plugins, and host conformance. Generated projects pin the Go module to <code>{sdk.version}</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Rust</th><td>ABI v1 bindings, typed results, and safe wrappers. The logger and all-hooks guests run through the shared host conformance harness. Generated projects pin the SDK’s exact Git revision with <code>git</code> and <code>rev</code>; this authoring path does not depend on crates.io availability.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table>
    <details>
      <summary>Want to build an SDK for another language?</summary>
      <p>Go and Rust are the ready-to-use SDKs today. For TypeScript, AssemblyScript, or another WASI language, a new SDK needs to implement Torana’s protobuf ABI, allocator, framed host calls, and hook contract, then run a guest through host conformance. Compiling to WASM is one part of that work.</p>
      <p>Start with the <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugin-sdk/blob/main/docs/WASM_PLUGIN_GUIDE.md">plugin ABI guide</a> and <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugin-sdk/issues">open an SDK issue</a> to discuss your language and approach.</p>
    </details>

    <h2>How to read this matrix</h2>
    <p>Coverage is endpoint-specific: matching a provider name alone is not enough. Use the format and harness rows above to choose your route and distinguish automated wire tests from credentialed client checks.</p>
    <p>Implementation evidence: <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/HARNESS_COMPATIBILITY.md">harness boundary ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/PROMPT_CACHING.md">prompt caching ↗</a> · <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-edge/blob/main/docs/BENCHMARKS.md">benchmarks ↗</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>
